(Bug #78: the buffer size bound lived in the parser's literal arm alone,
 so a size named in a variable escaped it entirely - and the arm that let
 the name through called itself "validated at compile time". The bound is
 now a rule about sizes rather than about spellings, which means a named
 size WITHIN the bound must still size the buffer in every spelling the
 manual gives. This is the working neighbour of the refusals in
 tests/compile_fail/173-178.)

a number called wanted is 256.

a buffer called first is wanted bytes in size.
Print "first: {first's capacity}".

a buffer called second is wanted bytes.
Print "second: {second's capacity}".

Create a buffer called third with size wanted.
Print "third: {third's capacity}".

Create a buffer called fourth with capacity wanted.
Print "fourth: {fourth's capacity}".

Create a buffer called fifth of size wanted.
Print "fifth: {fifth's capacity}".

(A size the compiler works out for itself is a size it can measure.)
a number called doubled is wanted multiply 2.
a buffer called sixth is doubled bytes in size.
Print "sixth: {sixth's capacity}".

(The literal spelling is untouched.)
a buffer called seventh is 512 bytes in size.
Print "seventh: {seventh's capacity}".

(So is the sizeless dynamic buffer, which parses to the same size-0 tree a
 literal `0 bytes` would and must not be mistaken for one.)
a buffer called eighth.
Set eighth to "grows as needed".
Print "eighth: {eighth}".

(A buffer sized from a name holds what is written into it, as ever.)
Set first to "hello".
Print "written: {first} ({first's size} of {first's capacity} bytes)".
